OP here.  I actually don't like standard Sudoku that much - pretty
dry.  As with many puzzle types, though, the excitement comes from
creating more interesting variations of them, bending the rules,
integrating ideas from other puzzles.  Sudoku is a major cultural
phenomenon now, much more so than Sokoban ever was, and it is not
against Nethack's character to incorporate things like this.

Nethack is also very much a puzzle-solving game, so I disagree with
the idea that puzzles like these are not "dungeon-crawlish" (as
someone else wrote) enough - playing Mastermind with musical
instruments isn't dungeon-crawlish, nor are Keystone Kops and a
hundred other silly references.  There is a lot of puzzle solving
going on in a typical Nethack game that doesn't bear much on D&D hack
and slash.

The nethack version of Sokoban enhances the original, adding the
aspects of boulders being destroyed, creatures getting in the way,
etc.  The ideas I presented for Nethack Sudoku would add their own
peculiar Nethack complications to the puzzle.

As far as justification - it's a twisted amusement of Rodney's to make
adventurers cough up hard-earned items, many of them magic, to find
and sacrifice on a floor in order to get through a challenge level (I
didn't mention that the items suddenly disappear when the puzzle has
been solved).

